			<div class="content" id="post-content-4855087">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><span style="font-size:1.3em;"><strong>Page 1</strong></span></p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is the first spacecraft observed by the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As in the first ever spacecraft they ever witnessed or just the first one they witnessed for this SCP? Confused by the wording here.</p>
<p>Considering this is a listpages SCP, I know there's more to come and the first page gives so little info, it serves as a good, 'wait, something isn't right&#8230;' page, if you know what I mean. Even without the knowledge of listpages, I think it's still just 'off' enough to give the reader the sense that something isn't right.</p>
<p>The clinical is a bit iffy, though. I apologise that I'm doing a more 'general thoughts' crit and not a true in-depth LBL.</p>
<p>Mainly the bits that bothered me were:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>new telescope parts like refractors</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like 'such as' would be better than 'like'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX has a frame in the form between a cylinder and an oblate spheroid.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'between a' is what sounds sub-clinical to me. I'm sure there's a better word to use there&#8230;sadly, I'm not that great at clinical tone either, so I kinda&#8230;can't think of a suitable replacement for that :U</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On the bottom side is a small outer room</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The adjective 'small' is what bothers me, as I've read somewhere that most adjectives aren't clinical.</p>
<p><span style="font-size:1.3em;"><strong>Page 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Immediately, I find myself significantly more intrigued by this page. I know you said you had problems with Page 2, but I really like it so far.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon arrival, SCP-XXXX-1 will make said star disappear by unknown means.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'make said star disappear' is vastly unclinical.</p>
<p>How does it make them disappear? By touch?</p>
<p>If so, 'Upon contact with SCP-XXXX-1, stars will immediately vanish. How this occurs is currently unknown.' would be better. But again, I'm no clinical tone pro.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the lesser quality of amnestics at the time, Ferdinand von Zeppelin did not suffer from complete memory-loss and had a few fragments left of SCP-XXXX in his subconsciousness.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That&#8230;is such a fucking cool headcanon you thought of. I love this touch. A lot. Zeppelins are the vague hint towards this SCPs existence. I think that's brilliant.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Currently, the LZ 127 Zeppelin represents SCP-XXXX the most accurate.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'Currently, SCP-XXXX is most accurately represented by the LZ 127 Zeppelin.'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On 28 August 1903, several researchers educated in astrology sighted SCP-XXXX near the Orion Arm. Upon showing von Zeppelin their findings, it was confirmed that this was indeed the same object he saw 30 years ago. He was then administered amnestics again, this time resulting in the permanent loss of his memories of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would just remove this bit. No way the Foundation would show someone an anomaly only to re-amnesticize them again. I don't buy it. Also, we already know that SCP-XXXX is confirmed to be what he saw, so this is unnecessary and repeats info we already know.</p>
<p>Page 2 is pretty good so far. Definitely a good jump from Page 1. I'll go ahead and read Page 3 and I'll let you know how it stacks up.</p>
<p><span style="font-size:1.3em;"><strong>Page 3</strong></span></p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is a dark metallic spacecraft roaming through space with one passenger, designated as SCP-XXXX-1 on board</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'SCP-XXXX is a dark metallic spacecraft roaming through space with one passenger on board, designated SCP-XXXX-1.'</p>
<p>Also, I really like this bit. This definitely wasn't something I saw coming.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>ALSO, HAVE YOU THOUGH OF A NAME YET?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Thought*</p>
<blockquote>
<p>YOU HAVE ALWAYS GIVEN ME THE SAME NAME.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>- GLEN, SCP-XXXX-1, THE RESETTER OF THE UNIVERSE</p>
<p>That was a twist and a half and I fucking love it and it genuinely gives me motivation to continue my own ambitious as hell listpages SCP.</p>
<p>Thank you for sharing this draft with me.</p>
<p>As for the falling action with the O5 sending Glen the godspeed message&#8230;idk how to feel about that.</p>
<p>I really like the idea of an anomaly that can't be contained and is universally recognized as good, but at the same time, idk if this is the most realistic reaction the O5 would have.</p>
<p>Either way, the implications, Glen, and the twist at the end is all superb.</p>
<p>Even with the issues I stated above, that twist at the end would have easily earned my upvote.</p>
<p>I think it all works as a whole, just need polish in all the right areas and you're golden.</p>
<p>I didn't stay to deeply examine the dialogue further, but it's definitely improving significantly.</p>
<p>Either way, I hope I helped. Good luck with the rest of this skip, dude. You got this.</p>
<p>👍</p>
</div>
