			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858296">
									

<p>We talked a bit in IRC for crit on the first two iterations. The third one is going to be a bit more in-depth.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Line&nbsp;by&nbsp;line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">That's&nbsp;just&nbsp;fine</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is presumably made from an alloy of palladium and black rhodium. The exact composition cannot be confirmed due to the inability of acquiring a sample.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This can be one sentence: Although it cannot be confirmed due to the difficulty of acquiring a sample, SCP-XXXX is presumably made from an alloy of palladium and black rhodium.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is the object that the first zeppelin blueprints are based on.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems out of place here, I'd recommend either omitting it (since that's what you imply with the von Zeppelin stuff later) or putting it with the von Zeppelin stuff. It just doesn't seem related to anything else being discussed in this section.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1 &quot;Glen&quot; is an entity whose age nor origin have been successfully investigated. It claims that these are untraceable because it is a being that was spontaneously formed in the universe.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a big deal with part of the reason behind the importance of clinical tone: The Foundation wants to do their best to keep people from getting emotionally attached to anomalies and related beings, so calling it by its preferred name here is a big no-no. I'd recommend this:</p>
<p>&quot;SCP-XXXX-1 is a sapient entity of currently unknown age and origin. During communication with the entity, it has claimed that these details are unknown even to itself, as it was spontaneously formed in the universe.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1 travels through space in search of dying stars. Upon arrival, SCP-XXXX-1 will collect the star and store it inside SCP-XXXX. SCP-XXXX-1 has been observed leaping several lightyears worth of distance by Beholder-3. It hasn't elaborated on its way of traveling nor how it can interact with stars. Its whereabouts are currently unknown.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This whole paragraph is a bit haphazardly constructed, and has some issues with tone as well. I'd go with something along the lines of:</p>
<p>&quot;The propulsion method employed by SCP-XXXX is currently unknown. It has been observed by the Beholder-3 satellite to travel several light years nigh instantaneously, and is nearly impossible to track because of this. SCP-XXXX-1 utilizes SCP-XXXX to travel through space in search of dying stars. Upon arrival, SCP-XXXX-1 will collect the star and store it inside SCP-XXXX. The entity has not explained the method of propulsion or the mechanism by which it interacts with stars.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Discovery: On 4 December 1872, Ferdinand von Zeppelin claimed to have seen SCP-XXXX with his telescope. He was administered amnestics, however due to the lesser quality of amnestics at the time, Ferdinand von Zeppelin did not suffer from complete memory-loss and had a few fragments left of SCP-XXXX in his subconsciousness.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We discussed this section while talking about its iteration on a previous page, the same comments apply here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Over the years, von Zeppelin showed more interest in air-travel. After internal discussion, the Foundation decided to allow von Zeppelin to invent new aircraft with undercover personnel beside him as manual laborers. It was anticipated that von Zeppelin would eventually develop a scaled-down version of SCP-XXXX. Currently, the LZ 127 Zeppelin is the most accurate representation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A few more issues with tone and structure here.</p>
<p>&quot;Presumably due to the remnants of the memory of SCP-XXXX, von Zeppelin developed a vested interest in air travel. After internal discussion, Foundation administrative staff decided to allow von Zeppelin to create new aircraft with undercover Foundation personnel acting as manual laborers in his employ. It was anticipated that von Zeppelin would eventually develop an aircraft based on the design of SCP-XXXX. Currently, the LZ 127 Zeppelin is the most accurate representation, though it is significantly smaller in size.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On 28 August 1903, several researchers observed SCP-XXXX near the Orion Arm and confirmed that this was the same object von Zeppelin saw 30 years ago by its description.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We discussed this section on IRC as well.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On 25 February 1955, the Foundation successfully launched the probes from &quot;The Beholder Line&quot; in space for further research of the universe. On 29 July 1958, SCP-XXXX was observed by Beholder-3 nearly 55 years after its last observation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think the quotes around The Beholder Line are unneeded, and it should probably read as 'the <em>Beholder</em> series'. We know the dates involved and can see it was nearly 55 years, so that part is unnecessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On 2 July 1962, &quot;The Diplomat&quot;, a new probe was launched in space towards Beholder-3 to establish a direct way of communication and give the following objects to the passengers of SCP-XXXX:</p>
<ul>
<li>Several syllabi and audio files of 57 languages.</li>
<li>Photographs of life on Earth, and music approved by several site-directors.</li>
<li>A transmitter, a receiver, and other equipment</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>A few minor formatting and tone fixes:</p>
<p>On 2 July 1962, the <em>Diplomat</em> probe was launched in the direction of Beholder-3 with the purpose of establishing a direct line of communication with SCP-XXXX, as well as delivering the following to the passenger(s):</p>
<ul>
<li>Audio files of 57 different Earth languages.</li>
<li>Photographs of life on Earth, and music approved by several site directors. (What music?)</li>
<li>A transmitter, a receiver, and other equipment</li>
</ul>
<p>The plural of syllable is syllables, not syllabi. Unless you meant, like, school lesson syllabi, which would be a bit odd since we don't even know if the thing knows our language at all. And why 57 languages specifically? You may want to look into the details of what NASA sent on the gold records put in the <em>Voyager</em> satellites. And what &quot;other equipment&quot; was included? Being so specific in certain regards leads to questions when things like this are left vague. Perhaps just &quot;deep-space radio communication equipment&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On 14 August 1986, SCP-XXXX-1 answered.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Slight tonal change:</p>
<p>&quot; On 14 August 1986, a response from SCP-XXXX-1 was recorded.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>14/08/1986 SCP-XXXX-1:<br />
ARE YOU THERE? BIST DU DA? ÊTES-VOUS LA?</p>
<p>| OTHER LANGUAGES OMITTED FOR BREVITY</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why have three languages displayed, but others omitted? How many languages did it reply with in total? All 57?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the distance and interference, it took each message one week to travel the distance between both parties, full communication logs available below.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>More slight tonal changes:</p>
<p>&quot;Due to the distance and interference inherent in deep space radio communication technology, each message took approximately seven days to fully transmit. Full communication logs are available below.&quot;</p>
<p><strong>The whole communication log:</strong></p>
<p>This part is definitely an awkward read. I don't know that I want to break it all down individually, but let me lay out a few problems:</p>
<ul>
<li>Something that barely understands our language is giving itself names like The Star Euthanizer and The Pilgrim Between The Suns? Those are not exactly low-level linguistics. Reading on, I can see that it has clearly learned English before, but nobody in the Foundation seems to pick up on this, when at this point in their communication, as far as they know, this is the first time a human has communicated with it. They're not suspicious of this at all?</li>
<li>You mention earlier in the article that details like how the ship's propulsion system works are unknown, but nobody seems to be asking anything about the ship here. I'd think that in the 80s, the Foundation would be pretty damn interested in figuring out the mechanics of FTL travel.</li>
<li>Why Glen? Is there any significance to that name? Why not send it a list of common Earth names and let it choose one?</li>
<li>The Foundation half of the communication feels very informal at times considering that this would presumably be a really big deal. For example: &quot;YOU KNOW THAT DYING STARS ARE HIGHLY EXPLOSIVE, RIGHT?&quot; &quot;, right?&quot; is really informal. &quot;WE DON'T KNOW A LOT ABOUT YOU &quot; should be something like &quot;WE HAVE LITTLE INFORMATION ABOUT YOUR STATUS OR HISTORY&quot;. &quot;THEN WHAT AFTER YOU COLLECTED THE LAST ONE?&quot; is another example. These are highly trained, educated, intelligent scientists communicating with an extraterrestrial in deep space, not a few friends catching up over coffee.</li>
<li>&quot;I AM THE OLDEST VACUUMBORN BY FAR&quot; he's so old that the concept of time itself is lost to him, but he knows he's the oldest one out there? That seems odd.</li>
<li>Him changing his title at the end of every message seems really unnecessary and is just sort of distracting throughout the logs. Didn't he ask to be given a name so that titles and labels wouldn't be used?</li>
<li>Why does something as old as time care about wishing humans a happy new year?</li>
<li>&quot;YOU HAVE ALWAYS GIVEN ME THE SAME NAME.&quot; This thing has reset the universe god knows how many times, but whoever communicates with it always gives it the same name? That's a hell of a coincidence, don't you think?</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>By an 11:2 vote, it is accepted that SCP-XXXX-1 is responsible for a ZK Universe Reset Scenario but does not directly pose a threat to humanity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All we have at this point is this thing's word. Even with the technology for FTL travel and containing a star before it goes supernova, there doesn't appear to be any actual evidence that it has the ability to reset the universe. If this was accepted by a vote, I'd think it'd be a much closer ratio than 11:2.</p>
<p>And at the end of it all, why would they not want to contain something with the power to reset the universe? How is that &quot;not directly posing a threat to humanity&quot;??? It may not be attacking us, but resetting the universe is literally the end of the world as we know it. That's not a threat?</p>
<hr />
<p>The overall idea here is solid, but honestly the article really starts to break down a bit once you get to the communication logs. There's just so much that the Foundation is willing to take at face value when the only solid information they have is pretty limited. There are a myriad of ways it could learn things like the term O5 that don't involve having communicated with the Foundation in a past iteration of the universe, and they just accept that too easily for me to swallow. Unfortunately, I'm not really certain how you'd go about fixing this. I also didn't get much of any emotional response from it, it was more of just an &quot;oh, that's what it's doing.&quot; The fact that the spacecraft was the inspiration for the design of the Zeppelin is given great prominence in the earlier iterations, but it seems to end up not really meaning anything besides a one-off line in the communication logs. This is an entity that claims to be as old as time itself and was willed into existence by the universe, and I seem to be more curious about it than the Foundation are.</p>
<p>I guess the best way I can wrap it up is to say that I feel like there's not that much of a narrative here - it's just a list of things happening and a conversation. There wasn't much that made me want to keep reading other than the Zeppelin stuff, and again, that didn't really seem to end up meaning anything.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

